Here's what was added.

### Server component (`app/page.tsx`)

Uses [`after`](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/functions/after) from `next/server` so analytics run **after** the response is sent and don't block rendering:

```1:18:app/page.tsx
import { after } from 'next/server'
import { logPageView } from '@/lib/analytics'

export default function Home() {
  after(async () => {
    await logPageView({
      path: '/',
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
    })
  })

  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Welcome</h1>
      <p>Thanks for visiting.</p>
    </main>
  )
}
```

### Analytics service (`lib/analytics.ts`)

`logPageView` POSTs to `ANALYTICS_ENDPOINT` when set; otherwise it logs to the console in development.

The production build passes. On this static route, `after` runs at build time or on revalidation (per Next.js docs). At request time on dynamic routes, it runs after the response is finished.

To send real events in production, set `ANALYTICS_ENDPOINT` to your analytics API URL.

